@doc """
Extract one successful Monad payload as a Result.
SafeBind (`=?`) calls this operation once after checking the input type.
An Err is passed to the current failure target; an Ok supplies its entire
payload to the Pattern. Nested payloads and child Patterns are not extracted
again by SafeBind.

Statement-final `?` repeatedly calls this operation on each successful payload
until reaching Unit, including nested values with different MonadExtractor
implementations. Every layer must have the declared capability, and its
successful type chain must statically reach Unit in finitely many steps.
An unknown terminal type is not inferred as Unit; Unit itself is not an input.
The input expression and each reached extract are evaluated once. An Err at
any layer retains its original Error and stops inner extraction and the
continuation; only successful layers ending in Unit continue. Each call's
returned Result is checked once before extracting its payload at the next layer.
Ordinary SafeBind and explicit extract calls still decompose only one layer.
`extract_error` uses the same one-layer extraction, returning the original Error
inside Ok. A successful outer layer fails with ErrorExtractionFailed, even if
its payload contains an Err. Flatten the layers you want to inspect explicitly.

The standard implementations are Result (identity), Option (None becomes
NoneError), and Either<Error, A> (Left retains its original Error). Existing
Errors keep their kind, message, Payload, location, cause, diagnostic and
stack trace. NoneError is created by Option's extract implementation.

MonadExtractor requires Monad, but is independent of MonadFail and Convert.
MonadFail receives an Error at the failure target; it does not extract inputs.
Implementing Monad alone does not provide this operation automatically.

SafeBind and statement-final `?` need no import. Qualified calls use
`MonadExtractor::extract(value)`; bare `extract(value)` requires
`import MonadExtractor`. This trait is not autoimported.

## REPL
xldr(1)> MonadExtractor::extract(Option::Some(3))
Ok(3)
xldr(2)> MonadExtractor::extract(Option<String>::None)
Err(NoneError())

## Statement-final question
xldr(1)> checked: (Result<Option<Unit>> -> Result<Int>) = {|target| target?; Ok(7)}
xldr(2)> checked(Ok(Option::Some(())))
Ok(7)
xldr(3)> Result::is_err(checked(Ok(Option<Unit>::None)))
True
"""
deftrait MonadExtractor
where
  Self: Monad
{
  @doc """
  Return the successful payload unchanged inside Ok, or an Error inside Err.
  The returned Result is the only layer processed by SafeBind. An existing
  Error must be retained without rebuilding it. The operation may explicitly
  generate an Error for an input failure that does not already contain one.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> MonadExtractor::extract(Option::Some(2))
  Ok(2)
  xldr(2)> Result::is_err(MonadExtractor::extract(Option<Int>::None))
  True
  """
  def extract(self: Self<$A>) -> Result<$A>

  @doc """
  Extract one layer and return its original Error inside Ok.
  Calls extract once. A successful input returns ErrorExtractionFailed with the
  fixed message "error extraction failed" and no saved Payload. No Eq or Show
  capability is required, and successful values are not inspected.

  This does not search nested payloads. Ok(Err(error)) has a successful outer
  layer; flatten the intended Result layers before extracting their failure.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> nested: Result<Result<Int>> = Ok(Err(NoneError()))
  xldr(2)> Result::is_err(MonadExtractor::extract_error(nested))
  True
  xldr(3)> flattened: Result<Int> = Result::flatten(nested)
  xldr(4)> checked: Result<Error> = MonadExtractor::extract_error(flattened)
  xldr(5)> match checked { Ok(error) => Error::is_kind(error, NoneError), Err(_) => False }
  True
  """
  def extract_error(self: Self<$A>) -> Result<Error> {
    match MonadExtractor::extract(self) {
      Err(error) => Ok(error),
      Ok(_) => Err(ErrorExtractionFailed()),
    }
  }
}

@doc """
The selected MonadExtractor layer succeeded instead of yielding an Error.
The message is fixed and the saved Payload is empty.
"""
deferror ErrorExtractionFailed() {
  Self(message: "error extraction failed")
}
